        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Paracas culture</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Homeland</span><span class="info-stat-val">The south coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>, in today’s Ica Region: the Paracas Peninsula, with the Paracas Cavernas tombs and the Wari Kayan necropolis on Cerro Colorado; the Chincha Valley, probably its political centre, with Cerro del Gentil; the Ica, Pisco and Palpa valleys and Ocucaje.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">About 800 to 100 BCE; pottery phases early (840–500 BCE), middle (500–380 BCE) and late (380–260 BCE); burials at Wari Kayan continued until about 250 CE, and the culture is thought to have evolved into the Nazca culture.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val">the Paracas Necropolis textiles and embroideries; mummy bundles; polychrome pottery with post-fire resin painting; trophy heads; artificial cranial deformation; the earliest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trepanning">trepanations</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>; irrigation; geoglyphs in Palpa province</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">How we know them</span><span class="info-stat-val">Excavations at the Paracas site from the 1920s by Julio Tello; later work in the Chincha Valley, including strontium isotope tests and radiocarbon dates at Cerro del Gentil; studies of the textiles by Anne Paul; skulls from the Paracas Cavernas; drone surveys of geoglyphs in 2018.</span></div>
