    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The Paracas culture flourished on the south coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> from about 800 to 100 BCE as a set of local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiefdom">chiefdoms</a> joined by shared religion and trade, with irrigation systems to raise their harvests.</li><li>Its dead were bound in a seated position and wrapped in many layers of finely woven and embroidered cloth, and the Paracas Necropolis textiles are considered among the finest ever produced in the Pre-Columbian Andes.</li><li>The Paracas culture is believed to have evolved into the Nazca culture, which shared its religion and carried on its textile making, head-hunting and warfare.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The Paracas culture shows the earliest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trepanning">trepanations</a> known in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>: parts of the skull were scraped and drilled away, and by the healing of the bone nearly 40 per cent of patients survived long-term.</p></aside>
    </section>

