    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">c. 800 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The Paracas culture emerges on the south coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>, within the Chavín sphere of interaction</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 550 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Cerro del Gentil in the Upper Chincha Valley begins to host feasts for people from across the Paracas sphere</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">500–380 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">In the middle Paracas period Chavín influence dwindles, and Paracas communities form their own identities and expand their trade</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 300–200 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">About 420 people are buried in two clusters of pit tombs at the Paracas Necropolis, wrapped in textile mummy bundles</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 200 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">A termination ritual with a great feast and offerings closes Cerro del Gentil</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1920s</span><span class="rf-text">Julio Tello makes the first formal investigations of the Paracas site</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1931–1933</span><span class="rf-text">Looters plunder the Paracas Necropolis, and its textiles begin to appear on the international market</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">2018</span><span class="rf-text">Drone surveys reveal many geoglyphs in Palpa province, now assigned to the Paracas culture</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Paracas culture was an Andean society that existed between about 800 BCE and 100 BCE on the south coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>, in what is now the Ica Region. It is named after the Paracas Peninsula, whose large seaside site was first formally investigated in the 1920s by the Peruvian archaeologist Julio Tello, and from whose excavations most of what is known of the Paracas people comes. What they called themselves is unknown.</p><p>Early Paracas communities lay within the Chavín sphere of interaction and formed their own versions of the Chavín cult. The first phase of Paracas pottery, dated 840–500 BCE and found along the coast and in the valleys, carries Chavín features: a feline-eagle motif resembles the falcon frieze of the Black and White Portal at Chavín de Huántar, the ceremonial centre of the Chavín culture in the highlands. Paracas emerged as a culture separate from Chavín while borrowing from its art.</p><p>In the middle period, 500–380 BCE, Chavín influence dwindled and Paracas communities began to form their own identities, building the religious framework that supported burials with many ceramics. The late phase, 380–260 BCE, saw pottery become more uniform and more people visiting the huacas of the south coast. Scholars order this sequence through the great burial complexes of Paracas Cavernas, the Paracas Necropolis and Ocucaje, built about 2,000 years ago.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="society">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Society</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Paracas had no central figurehead or government. It was made up of local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiefdom">chiefdoms</a>, joined by shared religion and trade but each keeping its economic and political autonomy, and relations between them were not always peaceful: battle wounds, trophy heads and obsidian knives are found at Paracas sites. Within the wider Paracas sphere, subregions grew up in the Chincha, Ica and Palpa valleys.</p><p>The Chincha Valley was probably the political centre, with the Paracas Peninsula perhaps serving mainly as burial grounds and Ica as a peripheral zone. Chincha has many roads, geoglyphs and religious centres where people from the coast and the highlands met, and large ceremonial mounds with little sign of permanent occupation, with farming and fishing in the land around them. Extensive irrigation systems raised harvests here and throughout Paracas settlements.</p><p>Cerro del Gentil in the Upper Chincha Valley, dated to about 550–200 BCE, was a platform mound with two sunken courts amid fields, used to host feasts. Strontium isotope tests of its offerings show that people came from long distances, suggesting that distant alliances were built first and on purpose. Most skulls from the Paracas Cavernas were deliberately shaped, mostly in the Tabular Erect form, while the Bilobate form was far more common among women; the shape seems tied neither to social status nor to kinship.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="beliefs">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Beliefs &amp; Worldview</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Ceremonial burial was of great importance. The Paracas Cavernas are shaft tombs set into the top of Cerro Colorado, each holding several burials and reused over the centuries; in some cases the heads of the dead were taken out, apparently for rituals, and later reburied. In the necropolis of Wari Kayan, hundreds of burials lay close together in and around abandoned buildings on the hill’s north slope. Each body was bound with cord in a seated position, most facing north across the bay of Paracas, and wrapped in many layers of fine textiles into a conical bundle, beside offerings of pottery, food, baskets and weapons. The dry coast preserved them.</p><p>Anne Paul suggests that the bundles’ shape, compared to a seed or a human head, was a conscious choice, the seed a symbol of rebirth, and that the quality of their cloth shows its ceremonial use. Textiles show figures falling or perhaps flying, some with face paint, holding severed trophy heads. Heads were taken in battles or raids, and the head may have been seen as a person’s life force, the place where the spirit dwelt. In the late phase a creature called the “Oculate Being” grew more prominent.</p><p>Some archaeologists suggest that an Andean view of gender in four parts, masculine and feminine combined, may explain why men’s and women’s skulls were shaped differently.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="arts">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Arts &amp; Knowledge</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Paracas Necropolis textiles and embroideries are considered some of the finest ever produced by Pre-Columbian Andean societies, and they are the main works of art by which the culture is known. Weavers used native <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> and the hair of the vicuña, llama and alpaca, wove on backstrap looms, usually in a single colour, and then embroidered the cloth. The earlier linear style followed the weave in running stitches, outlining nested animal figures in red, green, gold and blue; the later block-colour style filled curving part-human, part-animal figures with stem stitches in many vivid colours.</p><p>The dyes came from many parts of the Andes. Red came from the cochineal insect, ground with mortar and pestle; yellow from the qolle tree and quico flowers; orange from beard lichen; green from cg’illca mixed with the mineral collpa; and blue from tara. Making a dye could take several hours, and women spent two more boiling and dyeing the fibres before spinning and weaving.</p><p>Paracas potters applied a clay slip before firing and often painted afterwards with resin and pigment, making polychrome wares, with geometric, animal and human designs and a recurring full-face feline, that set them apart from Topará and Nazca pottery. Early and middle wares at Ocucaje used iron-rich reds and greens; late ones turned to a more neutral palette. Many of the geoglyphs of Palpa province stand on hillsides, where people could easily have seen them, and their figures may have marked territory.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="exchange">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Contact &amp; Exchange</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Paracas communities were linked by trade as well as religion. Their textiles combined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> with camelid fibre, and their dyes came from many altitudes, an example of the reciprocity by which people at lower and higher elevations traded goods. In the middle period they expanded their trade networks. At Cerro del Gentil, isotopes show feasters coming from far away, and offerings included bird feathers from far northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>. In the Chincha Valley, roads and ritual centres drew people from both the coast and the highlands.</p><p>The earliest Paracas communities lay within the Chavín sphere of interaction, and Chavín motifs shaped their early art. Conflict was part of life too: depressed skull fractures, probably caused by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sling">slings</a>, clubs and atlatls found in mummy bundles, are common in Paracas remains.</p><p>The end of Paracas has often been linked to an “invasion” around 150 BCE by the Topará tradition from the north, whose pottery appears at late Paracas and early Nazca sites. That view has recently been questioned. The termination ritual at Cerro del Gentil and at other Chincha Valley sites came before any Topará occupation and shows no sign of resistance to invaders, and the earliest accepted Topará site, Jahuay, was first occupied about 165 years after Cerro del Gentil closed. The decline of Paracas and the passage to Nazca were already under way when the Topará tradition emerged.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/trepanning"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/trepanning.jpg" alt="Trepanning" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/trepanning">Trepanning</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Trepanated_skull_of_a_woman-P4140363-black.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Rama, CC BY-SA 3.0 fr</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Scholars such as Helaine Silverman believe that Nazca culture and its iconography evolved from Paracas. Nazca shared its religion and in its early phases carried on Paracas textile making, head-hunting and warfare, and many Wari Kayan bundles hold textiles like those of the early Nazca; burials there continued until about 250 CE. Hendrik Van Gijseghem notes that Paracas remains are scarce in the Río Grande de Nazca drainage, the Nazca heartland, but abundant in the Ica, Pisco and Chincha valleys, and holds that Paracas settlement of the region and later population growth mark the beginning of Nazca society.</p><p>The Paracas culture also shows the earliest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trepanning">trepanations</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>, made by scraping and drilling away parts of the skull, perhaps to treat fractures. Judging by the bone’s healing, 39 per cent of patients died during or soon after the operation and nearly 40 per cent survived long-term.</p><p>The riches of the tombs drew looters. The Paracas Necropolis was heavily looted between 1931 and 1933, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-depression">Great Depression</a>, above all at Wari Kayan; how much was taken is unknown, but Paracas textiles began to appear on the international market, and most of those outside the Andes are believed to have been smuggled out of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>. In 2018 drone surveys found many geoglyphs in Palpa province, assigned to Paracas and shown to predate the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazca-lines">Nazca Lines</a> by a thousand years.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Paracas culture was a society of local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiefdom">chiefdoms</a> on the south coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> from about 800 to 100 BCE, joined by shared religion, trade and irrigation farming. It is known above all for the mummy bundles of its dead, wrapped in some of the finest textiles of the ancient Andes, and for its shaped skulls and early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trepanning">trepanation</a>. It is believed to have evolved into the Nazca culture.</em></p>

